kitty talk-thread survives a transient accept() error — applies to v0.48.2 (2cb1d95c3a) with `git apply`.
(git apply ignores every line above the first diff header, so this block is commentary only.)

Why (2026-10-01): kitty's remote-control "talk" thread died because accept_peer() returned false on a
transient accept() error and talk_loop did `goto end` (child-monitor.c:1821-1826, :2058 in v0.48.2).
Nothing restarts that thread, so remote control deadlocked. Evidence and analysis:
docs/research/session-durability-2026-10/C3-socket-robustness.md; build plan W3-build-plan.md § P6.

Part 1, kitty/child-monitor.c (local): accept_peer() returns false only for EBADF, ENOTSOCK, EINVAL
(the listener is gone). Any other errno is logged with log_error ("... still serving ..."), which
reaches the unified log where perror() went to /dev/null, and the thread keeps serving. A repeating
error backs off 2 ms doubling to 256 ms and logs sparsely, so an error that never dequeues the
pending connection cannot busy-spin the poll loop. The counter resets on the next good accept.

Part 2, kitty/loop-utils.c: the loop-utils.c hunk of upstream f3fdc21850d7 ("Fix #10436", kovidgoyal,
2026-09-04). The +/- lines are upstream's verbatim; the context is rebased to v0.48.2, where the two
CLOSE() calls share one line. A loop that handles no signals no longer clears the global signal
write fd, and handlers are reset per signal, so SIGTERM and SIGCHLD reaping work again.

Built without the title band by: scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh build --no-band (applies
kitty-upstream-defects.patch, then this file). Adoption is the operator's.

diff --git a/kitty/child-monitor.c b/kitty/child-monitor.c
index f969f98db..8804824ad 100644
--- a/kitty/child-monitor.c
+++ b/kitty/child-monitor.c
@@ -1817,14 +1817,31 @@ getpeerid(int fd, uid_t *euid, gid_t *egid) {
 }
 
 
+static unsigned accept_errors = 0;  // consecutive accept() failures; only the talk thread touches it
+
 static bool
 accept_peer(int listen_fd, bool shutting_down, bool is_remote_control_peer) {
     int peer = accept(listen_fd, NULL, NULL);
     if (UNLIKELY(peer == -1)) {
-        if (errno == EINTR) return true;
-        if (!shutting_down) perror("accept() on talk socket failed!");
-        return false;
-    }
+        const int err = errno;
+        if (err == EINTR) return true;
+        if (shutting_down) return false;
+        // Only a listener that is gone ends the talk thread. Any other error (ECONNABORTED, EMFILE,
+        // ENFILE, ENOBUFS, ...) is transient: returning false here killed remote control for good.
+        if (err == EBADF || err == ENOTSOCK || err == EINVAL) {
+            log_error("accept() on talk socket failed, listener gone, talk thread exiting: %s (errno %d)", strerror(err), err);
+            return false;
+        }
+        accept_errors++;
+        if (accept_errors <= 8 || accept_errors % 64 == 0)
+            log_error("accept() on talk socket failed, still serving: %s (errno %d, %u in a row)", strerror(err), err, accept_errors);
+        // Back off 2ms doubling to 256ms so an error that repeats (the pending connection is never
+        // dequeued) cannot busy-spin the poll loop.
+        struct timespec delay = {.tv_sec = 0, .tv_nsec = (1L << (accept_errors < 8 ? accept_errors : 8)) * 1000000L};
+        nanosleep(&delay, NULL);
+        return true;
+    }
+    accept_errors = 0;
     if (verify_peer_uid) {
         uid_t peer_uid; gid_t peer_gid;
         if (!getpeerid(peer, &peer_uid, &peer_gid)) {
